<!--
    A register the callee only pushes for its next call is no read of it
    (`option pushpadread on|off`, default on).

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture; every pass loads
    an in-tree x86-64 ELF through the CLI's own loader.

    `pushpad_x86_64` (gcc -O2, `show` built -Os) is the shape: `show` aligns
    the three stack arguments of `__snprintf_chk` by first pushing an `rdx` it
    never set, and `run` holds an end pointer in `rdx` at its call to `show`,
    a value it also compares. Pass 1 (`off`) reads the push as `show` taking
    `rdx`, so the call gains a third argument and `run` a third parameter
    whose only role is to be passed on. Pass 2 (the default) follows the
    pushed slot: the stack pointer moves back past it after the call with
    nothing having read it, so it is no read, and both are gone.

    `pushpadsaved_x86_64` (hand-written assembly) is the case the rule must
    keep: `keep` pushes its third argument, calls `helper`, and pops it back
    to use it, while `pad` pushes an unset `rdx` the stack pointer releases.
    `caller` holds a value it also tests in `rdx` at both calls. Passes 3 and
    4: the popped slot is a read in both arms, so `keep` keeps all three
    arguments; only `pad` loses its third under the default.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
